well joined by the chair of the defense  select committee Tobias Elwood who's in  Odessa in Ukraine this morning hello to  Mr Elwood it's good to see you thanks  for joining us um first of all why are  you there  well firstly to better understand what  is actually happening here we're at a  critical uh juncture in this War uh with  Russia and there's huge appreciation I  should say for the British commitment to  what we've done to help ukrainians push  back the the third largest army in the  world and but Putin is now humiliated  and there's huge concerns that he's  going to go more extreme he's going to  find uh more imaginative way ways to  raise the stakes and uh he's starting to  regroup the other side of the danipa  river and it's so important that that  message is said at the G20 as the Prime  Minister has been underlying that we  don't give up now that we don't sign a  deal that we don't step back but we're  able to flush Russia completely out of  Ukraine and that's certainly the strong  message that that we're getting here  myself and the defense Select Committee  do you think that President Putin should  have attended the G20 Summit in Bali  yes understandably why he didn't the  last time he turned up uh at a an  International Conference uh he was  humiliated I think by the Indian Prime  Minister and others who said what are  you doing in Ukraine so he sent lavrov  uh instead but ultimately uh lavrov is  just going to give us the lines that uh  uh Putin is spouting to say that this is  nobody else's business it is our  business because you know the Green  Ships behind me are not moving as as  they should do and uh Security in  Eastern Europe is everybody's security  if we don't flash Russia out of Ukraine  now how can Ukrainian economy move  forward how can the Sovereign identity  of Ukraine exist this is the time to do  it if we sign any deal now all Russia  will do is regroup to attack again in a  year or two's time it has to happen now  I thought a deal had been done as far as  the grain was concerned bread basket of  Europe of course to get it out of uh  Odessa and to parts of the world that  need it most what happened  yeah I mean but this shows that Russia  has continued to be setting the agenda  we've been following it this was  Russia's deal and they controlled the  movement of ships but actually this as I  suggested before should be an  international humanitarian Safe Haven  with the UN stamp of approval that  removes Russia from it you have you know  Ukrainian territorial Waters here  linking up to international waters in  the Black Sea that should have nothing  to do with Russia yet Russia is the one  that set this deal and only one-fifth of  those chips are actually leaving this  port meaning that the world Africa and  indeed Europe has not been fed on the  scale and of course that causes rises in  food prices knock-on inflation problems  elsewhere so we need to lean further  into this this is the moment now to take  the responsibility of this um a port  away from from Russia back to Ukraine  and the International Community  there have been some successes we've  seen what's happened in Curson and we've  seen that president zielinski has gone  along to um congratulate his troops and  also to offer uh Solace and support to  those that live then have lived under  Russian control for the last eight  months but Jen stoltenberg NATO  Secretary General has urged the West not  to make the mistake of underestimating  Russia you would agree with that  a hundred percent I mean firstly occurs  on is massively humiliating for uh Putin  they've had to withdraw across the  danipu river but the Russia still has  control of you know the size of Portugal  in Ukraine so this is far from over so  again stolenberg is absolutely right uh  to encourage NATO members to continue  providing that individual bilateral  support as Britain is doing because  Munitions and so forth weapon systems  they need to keep going the back the  battle is actually moving away from the  battlefield as I said with Putin  attacking electricity electric  electricity and infrastructure and so  forth trying to uh break the morale of  the of the Ukrainian people by you know  freezing them out this winter but at the  moment the actual uh there is a lull in  war fighting because there will be a  spring offensive and that's what we need  to build up and prepare for  although anzalenski looking at these  pictures has said that this illustrates  the beginning of the end of the war do  you not agree with him  no no absolutely I do because it's  showing that Russia can't uh hold the  line that uh the Ukrainian defensive the  incredible fighting will of the  Ukrainian forces  um are able to then push back but this  isn't the moment as I say uh to sign  that deal to leave Russia in control of  part of Ukraine it requires additional  effort it means another six months or a  year if you like  um of what we're doing now and the worry  is is that the West's interest  commitment that so far will start to  drain away we we don't have good form on  this if you look at all the places that  we've been to to try and provide you  know a Force for good from Libya Somalia  um Afghanistan Iraq Syria and so forth  we've actually given up and gone home  leaving problems there we mustn't do the  same here this is in everybody's  interest but particularly of course  Ukraine's that we stay here uh and see  this through  where does this leave us when it comes  to how much of our GDP we will be  spending on defense going forward we  know that Liz trust said that that was  going to be three and a half percent uh  when pushed Rishi sunak said to me  during the summer about  um two and a half percent although Ben  Wallace said that when he was at the  treasury he was forced to say that he  was overruled by the Prime Minister and  the ladies were Hearing in the papers  this morning as it could be less than  two and a half percent our defense  budget what would you say in response  yeah well we're slowly understanding and  appreciating why hard power matters why  a strong defense is absolutely critical  to defend our interests whether they're  around the UK or actually here in uh  Ukraine there is a symbiotic  relationship between our economy between  our prosperity and our ability to trade  around the world and the world is  getting more dangerous not less so we  drop our guard at our Peril that will  affect our economy and I hope that's the  message that we'll hear on Thursday from  the chancellor who he himself as former  foreign secretary recognizes the  importance there's also an absence of  International Leadership dare I say we  are doing much better at stepping  forward encouraging others to follow  it's tougher to do that if you don't  have the hard power yourselves to  provide that example  um we're hearing that the traditional  G20 Family Photos actually been called  off one wonders how much this Summit  might actually achieve but then you look  at the fact that the Chinese leader and  the U.S president have got together face  to face for the very first time does  that give you  um some sucker  I think we should always uh welcome uh  conversations between the two world's  largest Powers China and the United  States absolutely but I for one will not  you know drop my guard in in being  concerned about where China is going in  the long term and its symbiotic  relationship with Russia despite what  they say publicly in our lifetimes China  will become uh Stronger larger than the  United States militarily economically  technology technology and that's  important to understand as to how we  manage that how we actually defend our  International values and standards which  China is Keen to exploit so of course we  welcome these talks but don't you know  kill ourselves that China still wants to  uh is watching carefully what's going on  here because it still wants to lay claim  to Taiwan  um a final thought 